<description>
Recolor each 8-connected foreground object by whether its normalized shape is repeated or unique.

Input structure
- The dominant background contains several same-color foreground objects.
- Objects are 8-connected; diagonal contact joins pixels into the same object.
- Up to translation within each bbox, exactly one object shape appears once
  and exactly one other object shape appears multiple times.

Rule
- Group foreground objects by their normalized bbox mask.
- Recolor the singleton-shape object red.
- Recolor every repeated-shape object blue.
- Keep the background unchanged.

Key insight
- The recolor is determined by object-shape frequency, not by position or size alone.
</description>
<annotation>
{
  "type": "transform_in_place",
  "src": "same-color foreground objects on bg, grouped by 8-connected normalized bbox shape",
  "transform": "recolor the singleton object shape to red; recolor every repeated object shape to blue; preserve bg"
}
</annotation>
<generation_spec>
```yaml
spec:
  grid: 10x10 [observed]
  background: 0 (black)
  foreground: single non-bg color (8 in eval [observed]; any non-0 color ok)
  objects:
    count: exactly 3 8-connected foreground objects
    shapes: exactly 2 distinct normalized shapes (up to translation)
    repeated_shape: appears exactly 2 times -> recolored blue (1) in output
    singleton_shape: appears exactly 1 time -> recolored red (2) in output
    pixel_count: 4-10 per object [observed]
    bbox: 2-4 rows x 2-5 cols [observed]
    separation: Chebyshev distance >= 2 between any two objects (required for 8-connected distinctness)
    the two shapes must be visually distinct (not rotations/reflections of each other)
  output:
    same grid dimensions, background unchanged
    singleton object pixels -> color 2 (red)
    repeated object pixels -> color 1 (blue)

generation_strategy:
  approach: direct construction
  steps:
    1. Create 10x10 grid of background (0).
    2. Generate shape_A (the repeated shape) as a random 8-connected polyomino, 4-10 pixels, bbox 2-4 x 2-5.
    3. Generate shape_B (the singleton shape) as a different 8-connected polyomino, 4-10 pixels, bbox 2-4 x 2-5. Reject if normalized shape equals shape_A.
    4. Place 2 copies of shape_A and 1 copy of shape_B on the grid at random positions, ensuring Chebyshev distance >= 2 between all pairs and all cells within bounds.
    5. Fill placed pixels with a single foreground color (e.g. 8).
    6. Build output by recoloring singleton pixels to 2 and repeated pixels to 1.
```
</generation_spec>
</reference_puzzle>

